title: 'Temporal Equivalence Principle: A Proper-Time Domain Boundary in the Outer Solar System'
abstract: |
  The orbital alignment of extreme trans-Neptunian objects (TNOs) is conventionally attributed to a distant, undiscovered point mass. A deterministic alternative is tested under the Temporal Equivalence Principle (TEP), modelling the anomaly as a spatially fixed proper-time field gradient — a localized domain boundary — rather than a shepherding planet. Using the JPL Small-Body Database and the independent CODE cometary catalogue, two distinct dynamical populations are shown to independently isolate the same 60-degree sector of inertial sky. The resident extreme-TNO clustering survives a strictly measured discovery-footprint null calibrated against the characterized Outer Solar System Origins Survey ensemble, and lacks the resonant substructure and physical-element coupling required by a secular shepherding model. A novel transit signature is then isolated in long-period comets traversing this boundary direction: a significant orbit-reconstruction discrepancy, an in-plane orbital rotation localized to the perihelion element and weighted to the inbound trajectory, occurring without orbital energy exchange and persisting after regression of a REBOUND N-body planetary baseline, of observational leverage, and of each orbit's own published error budget — a decomposition that excludes dissipative mechanisms, such as asymmetric interstellar-medium drag, on the channel they must act through. The comets' aphelion directions themselves are then shown to dipole toward the same axis at the 10^{-4} level against a galactic-tide-aware null, a spatial datum independent of the reconstruction machinery. The secular alignment of resident boundary objects, the inbound phase shift of transiting comets, and the shared spatial dipole are consistent with multiple geometric signatures of a single macroscopic lapse discontinuity in the outer Solar system, favouring a temporal domain wall over a point-mass perturber.
